## Classical Dynamics

### Hamilton-Jacobi / Lagrange Formalism

### The Mechanics of Cotangent Bundles

### $$\alpha = p\ dq - H(p,q,t)\ dt$$

### $$\omega = d\alpha = dp\wedge dq - dH \wedge dt$$

```math
\begin{aligned}
\omega|_\gamma &= \dot{p}dt\wedge dq + dp\wedge dt\dot{q} - \frac{\partial H}{\partial p}dp\wedge dt - \frac{\partial H}{\partial q}dq\wedge dt \\
        &= (-\dot{p}^i - \frac{\partial H}{\partial q^i}) dq^i \wedge dt+  (\dot{q}^i - \frac{\partial H}{\partial p^i}) dp^i\wedge dt
\end{aligned}
```

#### $$\omega|_\gamma = 0 \iff \gamma:[0,t]\rightarrow T^*(M\oplus\R)  $$ is a stationary curve for $$A_H:=\int_\gamma \alpha$$.

### Legendre Transform
When $$H$$ is convex in $$p$$, $$\forall \dot{q} \in T_q M\ \exists ! p=p_{max}$$  satisfying $$\dot{q} = \frac{\partial H}{\partial p}(p_{max},q,t)$$.  This defines the (involutive) Legendre Transform of $$H$$:
```math
\begin{aligned}
\mathscr{L}(q,\dot q, t) &:= \max_p p\dot{q} - H(p,q,t) \\
A_\mathscr{L} &= \int_{\pi(\gamma)} \mathscr{L}(q,\dot q, t)\ dt
\end{aligned}
```
is the Lagrangian representation of the Action, where $$\pi: T^*(M\oplus\Reals) \rightarrow M\oplus\Reals$$ is the projection operator $$(p,q,E,t)\mapsto (q,t)$$.

#### The (Meta)Principle of Least Action

The Principle of Least Action simply claims that the Classical Dynamics of Nature Itself tends to select trajectories that minimize $$A_\mathscr{L}$$.

In general, this claim is false. But the set stationary curves of $$A_H$$ are always interesting to discover, and in most cases they contain the curves that minimize $$A_\mathscr{L}$$.  Locally, **the differential equations for those trajectories are typically identical**.

#### Quadratic Form Translation Magic, part 1

When $$H(p,q,t)$$'s $$p$$-dependence (aka the Kinetic Energy component) is a non-degenerate, symmetric quadratic form, we may represent it as a metric $$[g_{ij}]: T^{\*}M\odot T^{\*}M\rightarrow\Reals$$
with inverse $$[g^{ij}]: TM\odot TM\rightarrow\Reals$$. The Legendre Transform relates them:

```math
\begin{aligned}
H(p,q,t) &= \frac{1}{2}\ g_{ij}(q,t)\ p^ip^j + V(q,t) \implies\\
\mathscr{L}(q,\dot q, t) &= \frac{1}{2}\ g^{ij}(q,t)\dot{q}_i\dot{q}_j - V(q,t)\ .
\end{aligned}
```

Furthermore, if $$H = H\_B$$ has an additional component $$B(q,t)\in T_qM$$, a linear funtional on $$p$$, we can **complete the square** and recompute $$\mathscr{L}_B$$:

```math
\begin{aligned}
H_B(p,q,t) &= \frac{1}{2}\ g_{ij}\ p^ip^j + B(q,t)_ip^i + V(q,t) \\
 &= \frac{1}{2}\ g_{ij}\ (p^i+g^{ik}B_k)(p^j+g^{jk}B_k) - \frac{1}{2}\ g_{ij} g^{im}g^{jn}B_nB_m + V(q,t)\\

&= H_0(p+g^{-1}B, q, t) - \frac{1}{2} g^{ij}B_iB_j \implies\\
\mathscr{L}_B(q,\dot q, t) &= \max_p (p+g^{-1}B)\dot q - H_0(p+g^{-1}B,q,t) - g^{ij}B_i \dot q_j + \frac{1}{2}\ g^{ij}(q,t)B_iB_j\\
&= \mathscr{L}_0(q,\dot q, t)- g^{ij}B_i\dot{q}_i + \frac{1}{2}\ g^{ij}B_iB_j(q,t) \ ,\\
&= \mathscr{L_0}(q,\dot{q}-B(q,t), t)
\end{aligned}
```

We will contextualize $$B$$ (e.g. as a variation of a stationary $$\gamma$$, etc) in useful ways in the remainder.
**Both of the last two expressions for $$\mathscr{L}_B$$ are critical**.
